// PARENTHESES ARE A WALL, NOT ANOTHER TIER — inside `(e)` the tree is
// exactly e's tree, and no rule outside the wall may reassociate across
// it, however convenient the fold.
//
// Every wrong way to "simplify" parens prints a plausible number here,
// which is why the file exists: an implementation that treats `(e)` as
// bare syntax and lets the enclosing Pratt loop reassociate through it
// (the classic shortcut in a hand-rolled AST->IR rewrite, and in every
// "flatten single-child groups during parse" cleanup) turns
// `(2 + 3) * (4 - 1)` into 13 -- the flat reading -- while a version that
// hoists groups as if they ADDED a level of looseness gets 19. The truth
// is 15, and both wrong worlds run clean. The `%` line catches the same
// bug from the other side: `(2 + 3) % 4` is 1, ignoring the wall gives
// 2 + (3 % 4) = 5.
//
// `10 - (2 -% 3)` pins the wall against a SAME-TIER left fold: without
// parens that spelling is (10-2)-%3 = 5; the wall forces the right
// operand first and prints 11. And the last two lines are the honesty
// check: where parens AGREE with the tiers they must be invisible --
// redundancy may not move a value either.
//
// THE ONE-LINE BREAK: make parenthesized_expression splice its child
// into the parent instead of standing as a barrier (or mark the group
// with the enclosing precedence so the loop can reassociate across it).
// `p1` flips 15 -> 13, `p3` flips 1 -> 5, `wallsub` flips 11 -> 5;
// nothing rejects.

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, AllocError> {
    println("p1 {}", (2 + 3) * (4 - 1));
    println("p2 {}", 2 * (3 + 4) - 5);
    println("p3 {}", (2 + 3) % 4);

    // the wall against a same-tier chain: right operand first, wrap op
    // inside.
    println("wallsub {}", 10 - (2 -% 3));

    // redundant walls: these agree with the tiers and must change
    // nothing.
    println("redundant {}", ((2)) + (3 * (4)));
    println("deep {}", ((((1 + 1)) * ((2 + 2)))));
    Ok(0);
}
